    "id": "q03",
    "question": "Why did WLC-100 windows reverse by themselves on rough roads?",
    "answer": {
      "found": true,
      "answer": "Body vibration on rough roads made the motor speed oscillate, and the WLC-100 anti-pinch algorithm read those speed drops as a rising pinch force, so the window reversed by itself. The problem was missed because the HIL tests used only a smooth road profile, so it first appeared in production (37 warranty claims in six months). The fix added a speed-ripple filter with a 30 ms debounce and a rough-road profile (RR-04) to the bench.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "document": "LL-2021-07-rough-road-reversals.md",
          "text": "Body vibration made the motor speed oscillate. The anti-pinch algorithm read the speed drops as a rising pinch force."
        },
        {
          "document": "LL-2021-07-rough-road-reversals.md",
          "text": "Our HIL tests used a smooth road profile only, so the problem was never seen before production."
        }
      ]
    },
